   114	seeds with a seed-invariant `max_abs_diff` equal to the constant (to fp32
   115	rounding).** A validator that passes either run invalidates every validation
   116	verdict of the session (the session aborts). Shakedown 2026-09-20 on
   117	ml-beast: the tight run failed as predicted with `max_abs_diff` 1.0000002e-3
   118	on both seeds (engineering data, not evidence).
   119	
   120	## 2. Task-quality gate (plan 0.2)
   121	- 8 fixed clips, 8.000 s each (= one 352800-sample chunk), from the
   122	  MUSDB18-HQ **test** split via the public HF mirror
   123	  `roro128/musdb18-hq-flac` at revision `32fd4e850b45ea0b635be067b921661984b53029`
   124	  (lossless FLAC re-encode of `danjacobellis/musdb18HQ`; CC BY-NC-SA 4.0;
   125	  research use only; **audio is never committed** — `configs/sdr_clips.json`
   126	  records track, split, clip start sample, and the sha256 of every derived
   127	  wav; `scripts/prepare_sdr_clips.py` regenerates the pack deterministically:
   128	  the first 8 tracks of shards `test-00000/00001` in shard order that have a
   129	  usable segment; clip start = the largest multiple of 441 ≤ 30 % of the
   130	  track length, advanced in 1-second steps until the 8-s **vocals** segment
   131	  has RMS ≥ 0.01 — a silent-vocals reference makes SDR degenerate; the first
   132	  draft of this rule, without the non-silence scan, produced two silent
   133	  clips and was corrected before registration).
   134	- **Every compared operating point is gated, paired against A at that same
   135	  operating point.** Reconstruction policy: each 8.000-s clip is processed as
   136	  non-overlapping consecutive chunks of the operating point's size (1×352800,
   137	  4×88200, 16×22050, 80×4410) and the chunk outputs are concatenated (no
   138	  overlap, no crossfade). Short-chunk `SDR_ref` values are therefore NOT
   139	  quality claims about the model; the gate is the paired regression under the
   140	  identical policy.
   141	- Per clip and arm: separated vocals `est`; `SDR(ref, est) = 10·log10(‖ref‖²
   142	  / ‖ref − est‖²)` (plain, no BSS-eval projections — never presented as
   143	  MUSDB BSS-eval SDR) and `SI-SDR(ref, est)` (both signals zero-mean; `s =
   144	  ⟨est,ref⟩/‖ref‖²·ref`; `10·log10(‖s‖²/‖est−s‖²)`); stereo is flattened to
   145	  one vector; float64 accumulation; a perfect match (e.g. `SDR(A_out, A_out)`)
   146	  is reported as `inf`, and a paired row with a non-finite value fails the
   147	  gate. Computed (a) vs the reference vocals stem and (b) vs the saved
   148	  eager-A output for the same clip at the same operating point
   149	  (`SDR(A_out, est)` — output fidelity).
   150	- **Gate (common to every arm compared on speed):** mean over the 8 clips of
   151	  `ΔSDR_ref = SDR_ref(arm) − SDR_ref(A) ≥ −0.1 dB`. The per-clip minimum,
   152	  SI-SDR and the vs-A numbers are reported alongside; the gate is the mean
   153	  and stays the mean. Tolerance tiers remain the numerical-equivalence check.
   154	- Membership: the eight tracks are distinct and are canonical MUSDB18 test-set
   155	  titles (the mirror's `test` split re-exposes the upstream 50-track test
   156	  set; names checked). The KJ vocals checkpoint's training data is not
   157	  established by its model card, so the gate claims **paired implementation
   158	  fidelity**, not held-out generalization.
   159	- Non-finite semantics: `10·log10(num/den)` with `num>0, den=0` is `inf`
   160	  (perfect match), `num=0` is `undefined` (zero reference / projection). The
   161	  gate uses the `SDR_ref` terms only: a paired row whose arm or A term is
   162	  non-finite makes the gate ungateable → FAIL. Output-fidelity terms vs A may
   163	  be `inf` (A vs itself by construction) and are kept and counted, never
   164	  averaged away.
   165	- The gate runs in restart `r1` of each arm at each operating point, AFTER
   166	  the timed region (the driver's lifecycle: settle → gate → validate → time →
   167	  SDR); A's per-operating-point outputs come from the session's reference
   168	  step, so no extra model builds are needed.
   169	
   170	## 3. Graph-deployment verification (plan 0.3)
   171	"Zero graph breaks" is not accepted as evidence of full capture. For C1, C3,
   172	H (Inductor cudagraph trees) and B, E, G (manual graphs), record per pass,
   173	from the node-level nsys export of session 2 (`cuda_api_trace` +
   174	`cuda_gpu_trace`, capture window = the 5a latency block):
   175	`cudaGraphLaunch` calls/pass, `cudaLaunchKernel`-family calls/pass outside
   176	graph replay (eager launches), memcpy/memset API calls/pass, distinct kernel
   177	names inside vs outside replay; plus, for the Inductor arms, the
   178	`torch._inductor` cudagraph counters (`cudagraph_skips` and reasons,
   179	`TORCH_LOGS=cudagraphs`) and the cudagraph-trees recorded-graph count.
   180	Launch counts include runtime AND driver entry points (`cudaLaunchKernel`,
   181	`cudaLaunchKernelExC`, `cuLaunchKernel`, `cuLaunchKernelEx`; `cudaGraphLaunch`
   182	/ `cuGraphLaunch`). This nsys version's GPU trace carries no graph-id column
   183	even under `--cuda-graph-trace=node`, so GPU events are attributed by
   184	CorrId correlation: a kernel whose CorrId belongs to a graph-launch API call
   185	is captured; to a kernel-launch call, eager; neither → UNRESOLVED (counted,
   186	never assumed captured). API calls are windowed by the CPU-side NVTX pass
   187	range, kernels by the GPU-projected range. Fourfold classification per pass: (i) captured
   188	model computation (kernels inside replay); (ii) uncaptured model computation;
   189	(iii) external input staging (cudagraph-trees copies of non-static inputs
   190	into the static pool — `_foreach_copy_`/copy kernels/`cudaMemcpyAsync`);
   191	(iv) other uncaptured copies/memsets. Also reported: recorded-graph count
   192	(Inductor counters), graphs visited per pass, replay launches per pass, and
   193	compile/recording counter stability across settle → validation → timing.
   194	An arm is **fully graphed** only with positive replay evidence (≥1 graph
   195	launch in EVERY captured pass) AND zero uncaptured model kernels AND zero
   196	unresolved kernels in every pass (medians hide a defective minority); the
   197	captured pass count must equal the requested 50. Multiple graphs per pass
   198	are legitimate; staging (`_foreach_copy_`/multi_tensor_apply, direct-copy
   199	kernels, memcpy) is timed overhead but not uncaptured model work; bare
   200	elementwise kernels are model work.
   201	Arms verified: C1, C3, H (Inductor) and B, E, G (manual), from their
   202	session-2 traces at both operating points. No expectation is registered.
   203	
   204	## 4. Boundary-cost microbenchmark (plan 0.4; design registered, owner reviews)
   205	`fullmodel/boundary_bench.py`: chains of N **dependent** kernels, N ∈ {10,
   206	100, 1000, 2000}: (trivial) one Triton program loads ONE int32 element, adds
   207	1, stores it back to the same buffer — kernel k+1 depends on kernel k, and
   208	the final counter must equal the total launch count (exact-integer
   209	dependency proof); (elem37) an in-place value-preserving fp16 elementwise op
   210	on a 37 MB tensor, N ∈ {10, 100}; (gemm) alternating dependent GEMMs
   211	660×384×1536 / 660×1536×384 fp16 into preallocated ping-pong outputs with
   212	1/√K-scaled weights (bounded values), N ∈ {10, 100}. Modes: stream launches;
   213	one CUDA graph of the chain (replayed); programmatic dependent launch for the
   214	trivial chain (Triton 3.4 `launch_pdl=True`; `gdc_wait()` before the
   215	dependent load, `gdc_launch_dependents()` after the store; the PTX is checked
   216	for `griddepcontrol`), on a stream and inside a graph; real-kernel PDL is
   217	UNSUPPORTED through cuBLAS/ATen launches and recorded as such. Note: the
   218	triton 3.4.0 wheel's `language/extra/cuda/gdc.py` carries the pre-3.4
   219	`_builder=` signature and fails to compile under the 3.4 code generator
   220	(`_semantic=`); the bench therefore defines the same two one-line PTX
   221	intrinsics with the 3.4 signature (identical asm strings) — recorded here
   222	so the deviation from the library call is explicit. The elem37 chain's
   223	working set (37 MB) fits the RTX 5090's 96 MiB L2, so that chain measures
   224	the L2-resident rate, which is reported as the plan-1.4 L2 bandwidth input. Timing:
   225	unprofiled device time via CUDA events around the chain, median of 20
   226	repeats with min/max → `T(N)` per (chain, mode), plus least-squares slope
   227	and intercept over N. **`T(N)/N` and the slope are effective per-node service
   228	times INCLUDING kernel execution — not boundary costs.** A separate
   229	node-level trace of the same script yields, per chain segment (NVTX), the
   230	median kernel duration and the median serial inter-kernel gap; for non-PDL
   231	modes the serial gap is the boundary estimate; for PDL modes kernels overlap
   232	and no subtraction is attempted (pure boundary `UNRESOLVED`); the traced
   233	run's perturbation vs the unprofiled `T(N)` is reported. Prior calibration
   234	points to compare against: Hazy (H100 ≈ 2.1 µs stream, ≈ 1.3 µs graph);
   235	NVIDIA's CUDA 12.6 figure ≈ 0.55 µs/node is empty-kernel graph device
   236	service time (RTX 3060), not CPU launch cost. **Until measured, no
   237	per-boundary number is used anywhere**, and the ledger's X10 must not count
   238	kernel execution as removable boundary overhead. Any unavailable mode is
   239	recorded `UNTESTED(reason)`.
   240	
   241	## 5. Session structure, protocol, budget
   242	- **Protocol 5a**, reused from `arms/common.py::timed_run` (per-iteration
   243	  synchronize latency block of 50 passes inside the cudaProfilerApi range;
   244	  one free-running block of 50 passes outside it), **five repeats per
   245	  process**; per-process statistic = min-of-medians and median spread; per
   246	  (arm × chunk) headline = **median over 3 fresh-process restarts of the
   247	  per-process min-of-medians**, every per-iteration list preserved
   248	  (an explicit extension of `sm120` README deviation 5; spread = max − min of
   249	  the repeat medians; within-process and between-process variation are both
   250	  preserved). **Plan 1.2 matched comparisons** use `fullmodel/matched_run.py`:
   251	  two arms built, settled and validated in ONE fresh process, then their 5a
   252	  blocks ALTERNATE (a, b, a, b, …, five each) so every block pair is matched
   253	  in time and clocks; repeated in three fresh processes. Registered pairs at
   254	  352800: (C1, C3) the graph-break tax; (C1, C1D) the DC-filter repair
   255	  alone; (C3, H) the pad-and-batch delta; (C1, C1A) once, to record the
   256	  expected compile failure. The matrix restarts are additionally round-major
   257	  (r1 of every arm, then r2, then r3). Clocks logged in the background at
   258	  1 Hz with timestamp and GPU UUID (`nvidia-smi --query-gpu=timestamp,uuid,
   259	  clocks.sm,clocks.mem,power.draw,temperature.gpu,utilization.gpu -l 1`) for
   260	  the whole session; timed blocks carry wall timestamps.
   261	  **Matched-pair acceptance:** both builds OK, both graph gates PASS, no
   262	  capped settle, a JOINT settle of the actual alternation (counters stable
   263	  across two full alternation rounds), both validations PASS after the joint
   264	  settle, counters stable across every block, and — joined at collection —
   265	  SDR PASS for both arms' cells at that operating point; otherwise the pair
   266	  is ineligible with reasons.
   267	  **Compile/recording stability:** a compiled run is eligible only if, across
   268	  settle → validation → timing → SDR, there are no new graph breaks or
   269	  unimplemented ops, the unique-graph count is unchanged, and every Inductor
   270	  cudagraph counter (recordings, skips, non-static-input recordings) is
   271	  unchanged; all snapshots are preserved in the run JSON.
   272	  **Provenance gate:** every process verifies the msst clone (HEAD = pin,
   273	  clean), the checkpoint sha256, the registered config path and the
   274	  explicit `use_amp` before building; the session preflight writes ONE
   275	  immutable `session_manifest.json` (repo commit + clean tree, sha256 of
   276	  this registration, of every measuring file, the clip manifest, pins,
   277	  environment) that every run and every reference is bound to; a mismatch
   278	  refuses the run.
   279	  **Headline rule:** a cell publishes a headline only if restarts r1, r2 AND
   280	  r3 all exist and are eligible (5×50 protocol completed, validation PASS,
   281	  stable counters, manifest-bound, not a planted/traced run), share the same
   282	  effective variant, and the cell holds an SDR PASS at that operating point;
   283	  a failed required restart is never replaced by a later tag.
   284	- **Unprofiled timing is the primary result.** Session-2 traces are for
   285	  mechanism; profiled and unprofiled numbers never share a ratio. Profiling
   286	  perturbation (plan 1.6): A, C3, G at 4410 and 352800, wall unprofiled vs
   287	  `--cuda-graph-trace=graph` vs `=node`; >5 % inflation → every mechanism
   288	  number from that trace carries the factor as a caveat.
   289	- **Session 1 (≤ 3 h, RunPod secure-cloud RTX 5090, pod name
   290	  `melband_profiling`, image `runpod/pytorch:1.1.0-rc.154-cu1281-torch280-ubuntu2204`
   291	  = the pinned torch 2.8.0+cu128 / triton 3.4.0 matrix):** in this order —
   292	  setup (idempotent `setup.sh` + clip pack + nsys check); eager-A references
   293	  at both operating points; the two planted-fault checks; C3 operator
   294	  qualification per frame count; the matched pairs (§5, plan 1.2); the arm
   295	  matrix at 352800 and 4410 (three round-major restarts, SDR gate in r1 at
   296	  both points); C2 last. Budget guards (registered): a hard session deadline
   297	  (3 h) with a 15-min extraction reserve — a cell that cannot start before
   298	  the reserve is `UNTESTED(deadline)` and the session is INCOMPLETE, never
   299	  salvaged by weakening validation or timing; per-process caps (25 min
   300	  compile arms, 15 min others); an **aggregate C2 allowance of 40 min**, and
   301	  a timed-out cold C2 attempt is not retried at that operating point (the
   302	  remaining restarts are `UNTESTED`; a timeout means unavailable
   303	  performance, not evidence that C2 is slow). Every run's JSON carries
   304	  `headline_eligible` with machine-readable reasons; a headline per (arm ×
   305	  operating point) requires three complete eligible restarts with the same
   306	  effective variant (capture scope / adapter / compile mode), else the cell
   307	  is `INCOMPLETE`. Evidence extraction before terminate.
   308	- **Session 2 (≤ 3 h):** eager-A references + SDR at the sweep points; chunk
