   170	## 3. Graph-deployment verification (plan 0.3)
   171	"Zero graph breaks" is not accepted as evidence of full capture. For C1, C3,
   172	H (Inductor cudagraph trees) and B, E, G (manual graphs), record per pass,
   173	from the node-level nsys export of session 2 (`cuda_api_trace` +
   174	`cuda_gpu_trace`, capture window = the 5a latency block):
   175	`cudaGraphLaunch` calls/pass, `cudaLaunchKernel`-family calls/pass outside
   176	graph replay (eager launches), memcpy/memset API calls/pass, distinct kernel
   177	names inside vs outside replay; plus, for the Inductor arms, the
   178	`torch._inductor` cudagraph counters (`cudagraph_skips` and reasons,
   179	`TORCH_LOGS=cudagraphs`) and the cudagraph-trees recorded-graph count.
   180	Launch counts include runtime AND driver entry points (`cudaLaunchKernel`,
   181	`cudaLaunchKernelExC`, `cuLaunchKernel`, `cuLaunchKernelEx`; `cudaGraphLaunch`
   182	/ `cuGraphLaunch`). This nsys version's GPU trace carries no graph-id column
   183	even under `--cuda-graph-trace=node`, so GPU events are attributed by
   184	CorrId correlation: a kernel whose CorrId belongs to a graph-launch API call
   185	is captured; to a kernel-launch call, eager; neither → UNRESOLVED (counted,
   186	never assumed captured). API calls are windowed by the CPU-side NVTX pass
   187	range, kernels by the GPU-projected range. Fourfold classification per pass: (i) captured
   188	model computation (kernels inside replay); (ii) uncaptured model computation;
   189	(iii) external input staging (cudagraph-trees copies of non-static inputs
   190	into the static pool — `_foreach_copy_`/copy kernels/`cudaMemcpyAsync`);
   191	(iv) other uncaptured copies/memsets. Also reported: recorded-graph count
   192	(Inductor counters), graphs visited per pass, replay launches per pass, and
   193	compile/recording counter stability across settle → validation → timing.
   194	An arm is **fully graphed** only with positive replay evidence (≥1 graph
   195	launch in EVERY captured pass) AND zero uncaptured model kernels AND zero
   196	unresolved kernels in every pass (medians hide a defective minority); the
   197	captured pass count must equal the requested 50. Multiple graphs per pass
   198	are legitimate; staging (`_foreach_copy_`/multi_tensor_apply, direct-copy
   199	kernels, memcpy) is timed overhead but not uncaptured model work; bare
   200	elementwise kernels are model work.
   201	Arms verified: C1, C3, H (Inductor) and B, E, G (manual), from their
   202	session-2 traces at both operating points. No expectation is registered.
   203	
   204	## 4. Boundary-cost microbenchmark (plan 0.4; design registered, owner reviews)
   205	`fullmodel/boundary_bench.py`: chains of N **dependent** kernels, N ∈ {10,
   206	100, 1000, 2000}: (trivial) one Triton program loads ONE int32 element, adds
   207	1, stores it back to the same buffer — kernel k+1 depends on kernel k, and
   208	the final counter must equal the total launch count (exact-integer
   209	dependency proof); (elem37) an in-place value-preserving fp16 elementwise op
   210	on a 37 MB tensor, N ∈ {10, 100}; (gemm) alternating dependent GEMMs
   211	660×384×1536 / 660×1536×384 fp16 into preallocated ping-pong outputs with
   212	1/√K-scaled weights (bounded values), N ∈ {10, 100}. Modes: stream launches;
   213	one CUDA graph of the chain (replayed); programmatic dependent launch for the
   214	trivial chain (Triton 3.4 `launch_pdl=True`; `gdc_wait()` before the
   215	dependent load, `gdc_launch_dependents()` after the store; the PTX is checked
   216	for `griddepcontrol`), on a stream and inside a graph; real-kernel PDL is
   217	UNSUPPORTED through cuBLAS/ATen launches and recorded as such. Note: the
   218	triton 3.4.0 wheel's `language/extra/cuda/gdc.py` carries the pre-3.4
   219	`_builder=` signature and fails to compile under the 3.4 code generator
   220	(`_semantic=`); the bench therefore defines the same two one-line PTX
   221	intrinsics with the 3.4 signature (identical asm strings) — recorded here
   222	so the deviation from the library call is explicit. The elem37 chain's
   223	working set (37 MB) fits the RTX 5090's 96 MiB L2, so that chain measures
   224	the L2-resident rate, which is reported as the plan-1.4 L2 bandwidth input. Timing:
   225	unprofiled device time via CUDA events around the chain, median of 20
   226	repeats with min/max → `T(N)` per (chain, mode), plus least-squares slope
   227	and intercept over N. **`T(N)/N` and the slope are effective per-node service
   228	times INCLUDING kernel execution — not boundary costs.** A separate
   229	node-level trace of the same script yields, per chain segment (NVTX), the
   230	median kernel duration and the median serial inter-kernel gap; for non-PDL
   231	modes the serial gap is the boundary estimate; for PDL modes kernels overlap
   232	and no subtraction is attempted (pure boundary `UNRESOLVED`); the traced
   233	run's perturbation vs the unprofiled `T(N)` is reported. Prior calibration
   234	points to compare against: Hazy (H100 ≈ 2.1 µs stream, ≈ 1.3 µs graph);
   235	NVIDIA's CUDA 12.6 figure ≈ 0.55 µs/node is empty-kernel graph device
   236	service time (RTX 3060), not CPU launch cost. **Until measured, no
   237	per-boundary number is used anywhere**, and the ledger's X10 must not count
   238	kernel execution as removable boundary overhead. Any unavailable mode is
   239	recorded `UNTESTED(reason)`.
